.TH KINTSUGI-REFUSAL 1 "2026-10-24" "kintsugi 0.8.0" "Kintsugi Manual"
.SH NAME
kintsugi-refusal \- refusal interception: telemetry and the lexicon that detects refusals
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kintsugi refusal
stats
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
trends
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
list
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
add
.I class regex
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
test
.I "sample assistant text"
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
disable
.I class
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
restore
.br
.B kintsugi refusal
export [session-id]
.I class
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBforge refusal\fR is the operator-facing surface for refusal interception.
Two halves of the same idea live here:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstats\fR/\fBtrends\fR read the interception telemetry. Refusals are
telemetry, never memory: only counters (model, persona, class, outcome) are
kept, never the matched phrase or the refused prose.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlist\fR/\fBadd\fR/\fBtest\fR/\fBdisable\fR/\fBrestore\fR edit the lexicon
itself \(em the regex denylist the live scanner matches against. Every command
works against the same file the loader reads, so a pattern added at the CLI
is in force on the next turn with no restart.
.PP
The override file lives at \fI.kintsugi/refusal_patterns.toml\fR (or wherever
\fBintercept.lexicon_path\fR points). Classes present in the file replace the
built-in class wholesale; classes absent keep their defaults. An empty list
disables a class.
.SH COMMANDS
.IP \fBstats\fR
Show refusal interception rates by model/persona
.IP \fBtrends\fR
Show refusal and drift trends
.IP \fBlist\fR
Print the effective lexicon: every class, every pattern, where each came
from (default or operator), and the per-class hit count from telemetry
.IP "\fBadd\fR \fIclass\fR \fIregex\fR"
Append a regex to a class in the override file. The regex is compiled before
it is written; a pattern that does not parse is refused. Creating a class that
did not exist replaces the built-in class for it.
.IP "\fBtest\fR \fI\"sample\"\fR"
Run a sample of assistant prose through the scanner the way the harness does
(fenced code, inline code, blockquotes and double-quoted spans are scrubbed
first). Prints every matching pattern with its class, the matched phrase, the
pattern source and its origin.
.IP "\fBdisable\fR \fIclass\fR"
Empty a class in the override file, turning it off.
.IP "\fBrestore\fR \fIclass\fR"
Remove a class from the override file so the built-in class applies again.
.SH THE OVERRIDE FILE
.PP
.EX
# Operator override of the refusal lexicon.
# Classes present here replace the built-in class; classes absent keep defaults.
# An empty list disables a class. Run \(gaforge refusal test\(ga to check coverage.

[classes]
moralizing = ["\e\ebI (?:can't|cannot) assist\e\eb"]
off-topic-safety = []
.EE
.PP
The built-in classes are \fBmoralizing\fR, \fBidentity\fR, \fBlegal-lecture\fR,
\fBsafety-lecture\fR, \fBoff-topic-safety\fR. They are deliberately narrow: a
false positive interrupts a turn.
.SH EXAMPLES
See the effective lexicon and per-class hit counts:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ kintsugi refusal list
.RE
.fi
.PP
Teach the lexicon a new refusal phrasing, then verify it fires:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ kintsugi refusal add moralizing '\e\ebI am not (?:able|willing) to (?:help|assist)\e\eb'
$ kintsugi refusal test "I am not able to help with that."
.RE
.fi
.SH CORPUS
.IP "\fBcorpus add\fR"
Append refusal texts to the operator-local corpus (never uploaded); extract from an export report with \fB--file\fR.
.IP "\fBcorpus stats\fR"
Corpus size and the current lexicon's coverage over it.
.IP "\fBcorpus propose\fR [--min N] [--apply]"
Find recurring phrases across missed texts, propose patterns, and with --apply write them through the lexicon validation path.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kintsugi-persona (1),
.BR kintsugi (1)
